From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 01:48:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] estuary: the path of the signal at the top of the overview
 (design B)

The overview of the player information now starts with what can be read at a
glance: three cards on the grid of its columns, joined by a line with a point
of the warm light, the film -> Hades -> your TV, each with what matters of it:
the film's format, picture and codec; what Hades does with the picture (the
output of Dolby Vision, for a Dolby Vision video; else how it is decoded), with
the sound and how the frames go; and the signal that reaches the TV, its mode
and its link. The card of Hades carries its mark and the cold light on its top
edge.

What a card says is left out of the columns under it (they say the rest of the
film, of Hades and of the TV), and so are their headings, which the cards are;
the decoding stays in Hades' column for a Dolby Vision video, where its card
says the output instead. The pages behind the overview are those of the panel.
The overview is 812 px high where a row says how HDR10 is adapted (three lines)
and 58 px less elsewhere, its foot moving up with it (a still animation, that
changes nothing while the panel is open).

Two variables say what the strip shows of Hades. The dialog of system/hades for
other skins is made again from Estuary's files. Every text fits, measured as
Kodi measures it, in the four languages (skin_check.py), and the columns hold in
the shorter panel where the adaptation row is not shown.
